{"connection_id": "BC-3f6d67afa230448aeed2", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.13", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "fire tests each builder's work", "note": "The commentary moves from craft preparation and fire used to straighten a staff to the condemned entering fire; Paul says the Day reveals each person's building work and fire tests it, shifting fire from the final punishment to an assay of workmanship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fcc805ddf7e49a453051", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.13", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fire tests each person's work", "note": "The day reveals each person's work because it is disclosed in fire, which tests its quality. This gives a distinct Christian use of fire as testing crafted work, beside the commentary's staff-shaping fire and punitive fire."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f36e661679ead26c6c18", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.15", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "builder saved through the fire", "note": "The commentary's condemned person enters the great fire, while its craft scene uses fire to correct a stick; Paul says a builder's work may burn while the builder himself is saved, even as through fire, distinguishing tested work from final destruction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c7cd448482e0dc6a2700", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.7", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God gives growth after planting and watering", "note": "The commentary glosses the Rab family as bringing something through stages toward completion; Paul distinguishes planting and watering from God's giving the growth, a compact image of development that culminates through divine action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6e12b1cd5620fed37d16", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.4.5", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hidden darkness and heart-intentions disclosed at judgment", "note": "The Lord brings to light what is hidden in darkness and discloses the purposes of hearts. It gives a specific New Testament development of concealed knowledge becoming morally consequential."}
{"connection_id": "BC-34e8b57f5a95ccf5137f", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.7.17", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "what the Lord assigned and the path each person walks", "note": "Each person is to walk as the Lord assigned and as God called. The verse closely unites an apportioned share with a directed way of life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4c6c495351245c4f61d4", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.10.13", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the measure and field God apportioned", "note": "Paul refuses to boast beyond the measure of the field God apportioned to him. The passage uses an assigned measure as a real limit and vocation, preserving both senses in the commentary's قدر."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2250e471ec5746d5b1e8", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.3.16", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "scripture is profitable for instruction", "note": "The commentary presents the reminder conditionally in terms of benefit; Timothy says scripture is useful for teaching, correction, and training, making the ethical usefulness of instruction explicit in a Christian context."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7aab9f705b934195e663", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.3.16", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Scripture profitable for teaching, reproof, correction, and training", "note": "Scripture is called profitable for teaching, reproof, correction, and training in righteousness. It gives a direct Christian account of how a reminder can be beneficial rather than merely repeated."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4228cafb7d5b3ec9ee3c", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.3.17", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "person of God made complete and equipped", "note": "The stated result of useful instruction is that God's person becomes complete and equipped for every good work. This translates the commentary's prepared, finished artisan object into moral and communal formation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9fc7b1ed84d13d488149", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.1.26", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "lot selects Matthias after prayer", "note": "The commentary identifies the forbidden game as arrow-drawn allocation of shares; Acts describes the gathered disciples praying and casting lots to select Matthias, a communal appointment rather than a distribution game."}
{"connection_id": "BC-45060384083f018fbb23", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.1.26", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Matthias selected when the lot falls to him", "note": "After prayer, the apostles cast lots and Matthias receives the lot for the apostolic vacancy. It is a concrete Christian counterpart for lot as discerned communal selection rather than camel-game distribution."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3d9772ef5a72fb11162c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.17.26", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "appointed times and territorial boundaries", "note": "Paul says the creator determined the allotted periods and boundaries of the nations. The text extends measured creation into fixed historical limits, the same measure-and-end dimension developed from قدّر."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6c72ca5319cf136cdc07", "ref": "SBLGNT:Col.1.12", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "share in the inheritance of the saints in light", "note": "The Father qualifies believers for a share in the inheritance of the saints in light. It supplies the positive eschatological share that the commentary contrasts with having no share in the hereafter."}
{"connection_id": "BC-54e73cbd1d1605e2a3fd", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.1.11", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "inheritance received according to a prior divine purpose", "note": "The community receives an inheritance after being predestined according to God's purpose. It joins an allotted share to prior determination, a close counterpart to the commentary's measured portion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-efd9a136ac9a38317f06", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.2.10", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God's workmanship, created and prepared for a walk", "note": "Believers are God's workmanship, created for works God prepared beforehand so that they would walk in them. The verse makes creation a crafted making with a prepared direction of travel."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c80572bfcd37d8746cad", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.4.13", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "maturity and the measure of full stature", "note": "The community is brought to a mature person and the measure of Christ's full stature. It offers a Christian development of the commentary's phase-by-phase movement to a completed limit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7bd62a033c08e462e13f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.10.34", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "better and abiding possession", "note": "The commentary contrasts preference for present life with the better, more lasting Hereafter; Hebrews says believers accept loss of possessions because they know they have a better and abiding possession, spelling out that temporal-versus-enduring choice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-529704b5eb467e35ecf4", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.10", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "discipline for good and a share in holiness", "note": "God's discipline is for the hearers' good so that they may share in holiness. The verse joins staged parental formation, benefit, and received share, three elements the commentary develops separately."}
{"connection_id": "BC-65797b07d09e8eb4394d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.25", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "do not refuse the one who speaks", "note": "The readers are warned not to refuse the one who speaks from heaven. It is a direct continuation of the response contrast in 87:9-11: warning becomes deadly when its speaker is avoided."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b27c38547511f8b6b0f9", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.3.15", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hearing God's voice rather than hardening the heart", "note": "The exhortation says that when God's voice is heard, hearers must not harden their hearts. It is a close response-pattern counterpart to the wretched person who keeps away from the reminder."}
{"connection_id": "BC-17a56798766f87269865", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.4.13", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "nothing hidden from God's sight", "note": "The commentary's 87:7 says God knows what is aloud and what is hidden; Hebrews says no creature is hidden from God's sight and all is exposed before him, a direct counterpart to the hidden side of that claim."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ce68538abda5ea3c360d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.4.13", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "no creature hidden before God", "note": "No creature is hidden from God's sight; all are exposed before the one to whom account is given. This follows the same speaker, hiddenness, and accountability structure as 87:7."}
{"connection_id": "BC-45c72beb9f17e302aa77", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.25", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "not a forgetful hearer, but a doer blessed in action", "note": "James contrasts a forgetful hearer with one who persists in the liberating law and is blessed in doing. It gives the commentary's beneficial reminder a practical outcome rather than mere recollection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a90219a0114ee286eff8", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.1.38", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Aramaic rabbi, teacher, and Arabic rabb", "note": "The source commentary labels the ayah's رَبّ with root r-b-b and glosses a family of nurturing and completing senses; John's Greek text preserves the Aramaic address Rabbi and explains it as Teacher, a related Semitic authority term with a narrower use here."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1fa0e72efa28c76f6050", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.10.4", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the shepherd goes ahead and sheep follow", "note": "The shepherd brings out his own sheep, goes before them, and they follow. This gives a close living analogue to the commentary's leading staff, named for preceding the one it guides."}
{"connection_id": "BC-18c9fadab82065ace1e1", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.16.13", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Spirit of truth guides into all truth", "note": "The Spirit of truth will guide the disciples into all truth. It supplies a distinctly Christian form of the commentary's guidance motif, where leading reaches truthful understanding rather than a physical target."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8c77d67085712dd68342", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.19.24", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "lot for the un-torn tunic", "note": "The commentary describes a camel cut into pieces and shares assigned by arrows; John says the soldiers cast lots for Jesus' seamless tunic rather than tear it, an adjacent but distinct use of lots to allocate a possession."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4c0c2493235aa513f220", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.1.9", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "priestly service assigned by lot", "note": "Zechariah receives the sanctuary task by lot under priestly custom. This is a Christian-scriptural instance of a regulated sacred lot, to be read beside the commentary's sharply negative treatment of divinatory or gambling arrows."}
{"connection_id": "BC-39872218c3ef32b8f15a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.10.42", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "chosen portion that remains", "note": "The commentary reads 87:16–17 as preferring one share over another and calls the Hereafter better and more enduring; Jesus says Mary chose the good portion that will not be taken away, a close image of a selected, lasting share."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1f37162229562953ab89", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.3", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "whispered words proclaimed from rooftops", "note": "The commentary says God knows what is aloud and what is hidden; Jesus says what was spoken in the dark and whispered in inner rooms will be heard and proclaimed publicly, a specific speech-level counterpart that turns secrecy into disclosure."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a683ff71cbf58d04d980", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.16.25", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "worldly good reversed after death", "note": "The commentary contrasts preference for present life with the better lasting Hereafter; Abraham tells the rich man that he received good things in his life while Lazarus received bad, but their conditions are reversed after death, a narrative counterpart to the choice's final share."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8f49d4a88c9faac296e5", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.3.5", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Isaiah's crooked roads made straight in John the Baptist's preparation", "note": "Luke places the leveling of valleys, straightening of crooked places, and smoothing of rough ways in the preparation for the Lord's coming. It is a specifically Christian reading of the road-making image that the commentary hears in سوّى and هدى."}
{"connection_id": "BC-71fe7c71cc031a185494", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.2.22", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fresh wineskins preserve what they contain", "note": "New wine must be put into fresh wineskins so that both vessel and wine are preserved. Its point depends on the condition and fitness of worked leather containers, an apt counterpart to the commentary's detailed water-skin craftsmanship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-93f6983e9ab50286a7a4", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.8.36", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "gaining the world at the cost of life", "note": "The commentary says people prefer present life although the Hereafter is better and more enduring; Jesus asks what profit it is to gain the whole world and forfeit one's life, making the cost of that preference explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c0009f0bf8ba9885db4c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.9.48", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Isaiah's undying worm and unquenched fire applied to Gehenna", "note": "Jesus cites the undying worm and unquenched fire in teaching about Gehenna. This Christian reuse gives the Isaiah image an explicitly punitive afterlife setting beside the commentary's great fire."}
{"connection_id": "BC-92c044048091192ccf9b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.11.30", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "easy yoke and light burden", "note": "Jesus calls his yoke easy and burden light. This is a close Christian use of ease as the character of a divinely given way, beside 87:8's promise of being eased toward ease."}

{"connection_id": "BC-61262a501a5f89cd2d88", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.41", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "condemned sent to eternal fire", "note": "The commentary's 87:11–12 moves from avoiding the reminder to entering the great fire; Matthew's judgment scene sends the accursed into eternal fire, a direct eschatological fire counterpart with its own judgment setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9efcc057c6d3e940336e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.41", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "eternal fire prepared for the cursed", "note": "The cursed are told to depart into the eternal fire prepared for the devil and his angels. This is a distinct Christian judgment scene for the great fire, with preparation language that also recalls the commentary's crafted readiness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9980dfdbbd7f64475241", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.13", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "easy road leads to destruction", "note": "The commentary says God makes the way easy for the addressed messenger and then describes avoidance of the reminder and the great fire; Matthew calls the broad, easy way destruction-bound. The link is limited because the easy way has a different referent, but the contrast makes ease point toward opposite outcomes."}
{"connection_id": "BC-18f3acfea1ca4e11db24", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.14", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "hard way leads to life", "note": "The commentary associates divine facilitation with ease and sets successful guidance against the fire; Matthew says the narrow, hard way leads to life, an explicit reversal of the easy/destructive route in the neighboring verse, though the addressees and settings differ."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2a538d865161f7a2f177", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.2", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the measure used returns to the measurer", "note": "The measure used in judgment is measured back to the judge. It turns the commentary's measuring vocabulary into an ethical rule of reciprocal assessment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-46cd7221a7bb2c068093", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.1.6", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "begun work brought to completion", "note": "The one who began a good work will bring it to completion. Its beginning-to-finish divine workmanship closely parallels the commentary's account of ربّ as completing what is gradually made."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5523abbccc60fc6682f4", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.11.1", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "measuring reed or rod for temple, altar, and worshipers", "note": "John is given a reed like a rod and commanded to measure the temple, altar, and worshipers. The passage preserves the joined material images of a staff-like implement and exact measurement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e880e33f6ebf497c85af", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.21.15", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "golden measuring rod for city, gates, and wall", "note": "The angel holds a golden measuring rod to measure the city, its gates, and its wall. This provides a second, eschatological scene in which a rod and exact measurement belong together."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0dc9be07f72239b88a6f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.21.8", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wicked people's allotted portion in the burning lake", "note": "The listed wicked receive their portion in the lake burning with fire and sulfur. It brings together the commentary's two final terms, allotted share and the great fire, in one eschatological sentence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8c4dc2046d41f5ed1b4f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.12.3", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "measure of faith allotted to each", "note": "The commentary's source gloss gives قدر the sense of setting a measure and each person's share; Paul says God has allotted each a measure of faith, a direct Christian example of individualized measure without claiming identical doctrine."}
{"connection_id": "BC-284b750dd7896fc7f799", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.12.3", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God apportions a measure of faith to each", "note": "Each person is to think according to the measure of faith God apportioned. The verse turns the commentary's measure-and-share vocabulary into a differentiated divine gift rather than a prize won by chance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-547103950e056f3d9497", "ref": "1QS 3:17-19", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "two spirits appointed for people to walk in until visitation", "note": "The Community Rule says God appoints two spirits in which humans walk until the time of visitation. Its measured duration and directed paths make a specific Second Temple comparison for the commentary's divine determination, guidance, and consequential choice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0eb02f36b7856dd588b4", "ref": "Berakhot 33b", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "everything in Heaven's hands except fear of Heaven", "note": "The Talmud's maxim reserves fear of Heaven from what is in Heaven's hands. It places 87:10's fear within a Jewish distinction between divine determination and responsible response."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8c641b5f7e238b7b483b", "ref": "Genesis Rabbah 10:6", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "a heavenly mazal tells each blade of grass to grow", "note": "The midrash says no blade of grass lacks a heavenly mazal that strikes it and says, Grow. It develops divine guidance of created life at the creaturely level, giving a Jewish interpretive analogue to قدر then هدى."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c0c47374334bcfa7414a", "ref": "Genesis Rabbah 1:1", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Torah as plan consulted before creation", "note": "The commentary reads creation as craft with planning before shaping; Genesis Rabbah compares a king consulting an architect's plan before building a palace with God consulting Torah before creating the world, supplying a Jewish interpretation of creation as planned workmanship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9f9f4474a9f488c64767", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 3:15", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "all foreseen, yet freedom of choice given", "note": "Avot holds together divine foreknowledge and granted human choice. This is a concise Jewish formulation for reading the commentary's measured determination without collapsing its final moral choosing into fatalism."}
{"connection_id": "BC-96615b32a5ed66c6c311", "ref": "Mishnah Sanhedrin 3:3", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "dice-player disqualified from testimony", "note": "The Mishnah lists a player with dice among people disqualified from giving testimony. Its legal suspicion of gaming supplies a Jewish ethical counterpart to the commentary's contrast between apportioned share and maysir."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0b968fe54eae4e180398", "ref": "Mishnah Shabbat 23:2", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "lots for household portions; prohibition of gambling with others", "note": "The Mishnah permits household members to determine portions by lot but forbids doing so with outsiders as gambling. Its legal distinction directly illuminates the commentary's double sense of maysir as distribution of portions and condemned game."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3c1c870a40af19dc0824", "ref": "Niddah 16b", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "traits allotted at conception but not righteousness or wickedness", "note": "The Talmud imagines an angel asking what a conceived drop will become—strong or weak, wise or foolish, rich or poor—while excluding righteous or wicked. It is a precise Jewish qualification of measured human endowment that preserves the moral choice foregrounded by the commentary's final contrast."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7c36243f9faa520098f8", "ref": "Pirkei Avot 4:16", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "this world as a vestibule before the world to come", "note": "The commentary says people prefer this life although the Hereafter is better and more enduring; Avot calls this world a vestibule before the world to come and urges preparation for the latter, giving the preference a Jewish ethical formulation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fc409bfc3b2c35d0c56c", "ref": "WLC:1Sam.16.7", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God sees the heart rather than appearance", "note": "The commentary says God knows what is public and what is hidden; Samuel contrasts what humans see outwardly with YHWH's regard for the heart, specifying inward knowledge against visible appearance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-58bf10fe00d529b8eeb4", "ref": "WLC:1Sam.16.7", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "humans see appearance while the Lord sees the heart", "note": "The Lord distinguishes human sight of outward appearance from his own sight of the heart. It is a particularly concrete counterpart to knowing the public and the concealed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-968d01333e65972e32fd", "ref": "WLC:1Sam.20.37", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "an arrow beyond the boy signals David's course", "note": "Jonathan's cry that the arrow is beyond the boy functions as a directional signal to the hidden David. This adds a narrative use in which an arrow tells someone where danger and movement lie."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9edcc33d369f23e987c7", "ref": "WLC:2Kgs.13.17", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "arrow of the Lord's victory shot toward the east", "note": "Elisha orders an eastward shot and calls it the Lord's arrow of victory. It gives the fashioned arrow a prophetic target and direction, like the commentary's hearing of guidance through an arrowhead aimed ahead."}
{"connection_id": "BC-903b32fcd6efe31d82ca", "ref": "WLC:Amos.7.7", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "plumb line set against a built wall", "note": "The Lord stands by a wall made with a plumb line and holds the line himself. This supplies an Israelite image of measuring straightness that turns the commentary's artisanal measuring into an evaluative act."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9e22d3190b7cbf41000c", "ref": "WLC:Dan.2.22", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God knows hidden things and darkness", "note": "God reveals deep and hidden things and knows what is in darkness. This is a direct counterpart to 87:7's knowledge of what is openly spoken and what remains concealed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b54cb272a4d093508ab1", "ref": "WLC:Deut.18.3", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fixed animal portions for the priest", "note": "The commentary describes slaughtering a camel and sharing its pieces by game arrows, then contrasts that with a measured share from God; Deuteronomy assigns the priest the shoulder, cheeks, and stomach from an ox or sheep, a specific animal share fixed by law rather than chance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d85ddf0d3ed703064c8c", "ref": "WLC:Deut.30.19", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "choice between life and death", "note": "The commentary calls 87:16–17 a choice of share between present life and the better Hereafter; Moses sets life and death before Israel and urges them to choose life, turning choice into an ethical decision with consequences."}
