{"connection_id": "BC-b5b69d9940b949f9cba6", "ref": "WLC:Prov.16.33", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "lot cast, decision from YHWH", "note": "The commentary treats the game arrows as assigning shares by chance and contrasts them with the share set by God; Proverbs says a lot is cast but its decision belongs to YHWH, so the lot is not an independent force."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9d7868cb324fb00e560b", "ref": "WLC:Prov.16.33", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "lot cast, but its decision from the Lord", "note": "Proverbs says a lot is cast into the lap while every judgment of it comes from the Lord. Against the commentary's ban on divinatory arrows, it supplies a contrasting Israelite maxim that assigns a cast lot's outcome to divine decision."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bd297f6c19cd2f007472", "ref": "WLC:Prov.16.9", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "human planning and divine direction", "note": "The commentary's artisan first thinks through how to prepare and straighten an object, then turns to guidance; Proverbs says a person plans a way but YHWH establishes the steps, a close sequence of planning followed by direction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-28b115429ca46266efb9", "ref": "WLC:Prov.16.9", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "human planning and the Lord establishing steps", "note": "A person's heart plans a way, but the Lord establishes the steps. This compresses the commentary's pondering of how to prepare a matter and the subsequent divine directing of its course."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7c9f8253f3ef98b08dce", "ref": "WLC:Prov.3.6", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "crooked ways made straight", "note": "The commentary's arrow-making scene joins straightening what is bent to directing its point; Proverbs says YHWH makes a person's paths straight, bringing straightness and guidance together in a path image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8f75657a06ae0d27a93d", "ref": "WLC:Prov.4.11", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "teaching a way of wisdom and leading in upright tracks", "note": "The parental teacher says, I have taught you the way of wisdom and led you in upright tracks. Its joined teaching, leading, and straight path provide a sapiential counterpart to measured preparation followed by guidance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-677d4eae79c54f2e9447", "ref": "WLC:Prov.4.15", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "avoid, turn away from, and pass beyond the wicked path", "note": "The father repeatedly commands avoidance of the wicked path: do not enter it, turn away, and pass on. Its verbs give a concrete path-image for the commentary's yatajannabuha, even though the object there is the reminder."}
{"connection_id": "BC-23461603dafb8f451581", "ref": "WLC:Prov.8.29", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the sea's limit and earth's foundations marked out", "note": "Wisdom recalls God assigning the sea a limit so its waters do not transgress and marking the earth's foundations. It specifies measurement as a boundary that guides created elements in their proper course."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f0fab47cc11f71447c61", "ref": "WLC:Prov.9.8", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "reproof helps the wise but provokes the scoffer", "note": "Reproof is hated by a scoffer but loved by the wise. It gives a close sapiential analogue to the commentary's conditional reminder: the same admonition benefits a receptive person and is rejected by another."}
{"connection_id": "BC-508072e71aea8423f136", "ref": "WLC:Ps.119.83", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "a wineskin in smoke and remembrance", "note": "The commentary separately describes a waterbag becoming smooth with use and a reminder that may benefit; the psalmist likens himself to a wineskin in smoke yet says he has not forgotten God's statutes, joining a worn vessel image to remembering, though the psalm's image is distress rather than ordinary use."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5551448b0858117dfc07", "ref": "WLC:Ps.119.83", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wineskin in smoke that does not forget statutes", "note": "The speaker has become like a wineskin in smoke but does not forget God's statutes. It tightly joins the commentary's weathered leather vessel with its later concern for a remembrance that remains effective."}
{"connection_id": "BC-73a3ba16715bd14b910a", "ref": "WLC:Ps.127.4", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sons compared to arrows in a warrior's hand", "note": "The commentary develops a shaped arrow and a quiver-like container; the psalm compares sons of youth to arrows in a warrior's hand, adding a family image in which the prepared arrow represents offspring ready for action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bbc10a46d5f4f49b2e46", "ref": "WLC:Ps.139.16", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "unformed days recorded beforehand", "note": "The commentary says every thing has an amount and term; the psalmist says his unformed substance and all his days were seen and written before they arrived, joining formation to a measured lifespan."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8ecc86e853dccd76fef8", "ref": "WLC:Ps.139.4", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God knows a word before it is spoken", "note": "The commentary names both what is aloud and what is hidden as known to God; the psalmist says God knows his word before it reaches his tongue, extending the claim to speech not yet made public."}
{"connection_id": "BC-923f08f804e70d2a2b85", "ref": "WLC:Ps.147.4", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "stars counted and named", "note": "The commentary connects creation with measuring and assigning each thing its share; the psalm says God counts the stars and calls them by name, a concrete image of creation known and ordered individually."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bdb70e74163afa0dc4c7", "ref": "WLC:Ps.16.5", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the Lord as portion, cup, and lot", "note": "The commentary distinguishes the game's allotted share from the share given by the measure-setter; the psalmist calls YHWH his portion, cup, and lot, recasting allotment as trust in God rather than a game."}
{"connection_id": "BC-20577fab351716a84a44", "ref": "WLC:Ps.16.6", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "measured boundary lines and secure inheritance", "note": "The commentary says an arrow-game share is measured out and contrasts it with the enduring Hereafter; the psalmist's boundary lines fall in pleasant places and his inheritance is secure, a fixed inheritance rather than a chance stake."}
{"connection_id": "BC-15af7f31b28c010f72d4", "ref": "WLC:Ps.16.6", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God as the speaker's portion and holder of his lot", "note": "The psalm calls the Lord the speaker's portion and cup and says that God holds his lot. It provides a theological alternative to a self-sought gambling share: the decisive portion is received from God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e3c15ce42f56ac83dc06", "ref": "WLC:Ps.22.19", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "garments divided and clothing allotted by lot", "note": "The commentary's game divides a slaughtered camel into portions chosen by arrows; the psalm describes people dividing garments and casting lots for clothing, a specific allocation scene without the game's animal shares."}
{"connection_id": "BC-34cdd3017e7b9932458a", "ref": "WLC:Ps.23.3", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "restored and led on right paths", "note": "The commentary links the Lord's completing care with guidance and describes a staff that goes ahead of its bearer; the psalm's shepherd restores the speaker and leads him on right paths, combining care and direction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7d39189241b7ee37c0de", "ref": "WLC:Ps.97.9", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew עליון and Arabic al-aʿlā, the Most High", "note": "The commentary's ayah calls God al-Aʿlā and notes that the same Arabic root names the highest game arrow; the psalm calls YHWH Most High over all the earth, so a related Semitic height term belongs to divine supremacy here, not a gaming share."}
{"connection_id": "BC-142a63e5799ca94c8406", "ref": "WLC:Zech.7.11", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "refusal to listen, turned shoulder, and stopped ears", "note": "The ancestors refuse to pay attention, turn a stubborn shoulder, and make their ears too heavy to hear prophetic instruction. This is a close narrative profile of keeping away from a reminder."}
{"connection_id": "BC-472c6684b36ffd09331f", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 11:20", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "measure, number, and weight", "note": "The commentary links خلق and قدر as making and setting a measure; Wisdom says God ordered all things by measure, number, and weight, an unusually exact Jewish formulation of creation as measured order."}
{"connection_id": "BC-22578dbb1e3c65b7b0a4", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 11:20", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "measure, number, and weight", "note": "Wisdom says God arranged all things by measure, number, and weight. This gives an explicit Jewish formulation of the commentary's claim that created things have a determined measure."}

